guidebook.section.mob.minotaur.name=Minotaur
guidebook.section.mob.mazeslime.name=Maze Slime

# The dark tower's six, and all six strings are the donor's own TFCreatures.registerTFCreature
# arguments rather than anything invented here -- "Redscale Broodling" in particular is not a name
# anybody would guess from the class, which is EntityTFTowerBroodling.
#
# ⚠️ The Ur-Ghast is registered upstream as "Tower Boss", which is a placeholder: the class is
# EntityTFTowerBoss, nothing in the archive spawns it, and no lang file, achievement or guidebook
# entry anywhere in the mod ever shows the string. The name every player of this mod knows the mob by
# is the Ur-Ghast, and that is what a nameplate should read. This is the one string on this list that
# is not a transcription, and the entity id (urghast) is named for the same reason.
guidebook.section.mob.towerghast.name=Tower Ghast
guidebook.section.mob.urghast.name=Ur-Ghast
guidebook.section.mob.minighast.name=Mini Ghast
guidebook.section.mob.towerbroodling.name=Redscale Broodling
guidebook.section.mob.towergolem.name=Tower Golem
guidebook.section.mob.towertermite.name=Tower Termite

# The two projectiles. Upstream never names them -- they are not registered as mobs at all, only as
# network-visible entities -- so these are this port's own, and exist because BTA asks every
# registered entity for a display name whether or not anything shows it.
guidebook.section.mob.projectile.name=Magic Bolt
guidebook.section.mob.naturebolt.name=Nature Bolt

# --- Blocks -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The log and leaf names are upstream's three metadata variants, which this port registers as
# separate blocks -- so the names carry over one for one.
tile.twilightforest.log.twilight_oak.name=Twilight Oak Log
tile.twilightforest.log.canopy.name=Canopy Tree Log
tile.twilightforest.log.mangrove.name=Mangrove Log
tile.twilightforest.leaves.twilight_oak.name=Twilight Oak Leaves
tile.twilightforest.leaves.canopy.name=Canopy Tree Leaves
tile.twilightforest.leaves.mangrove.name=Mangrove Leaves

# --- From the 1.15.4 donor. Benimatic's own strings, out of its lang/en_US.xml. ---
# tile.TFHedge.1 is "Darkwood Leaves" -- so even upstream calls the hedge variant leaves in the UI,
# while building it out of the hedge block. The name is his; the block choice is documented on
# BlockLogicTFHedge.harmless.
#
# ⚠️ ONE DEVIATION: upstream's tile.TFLog.3 is "Darkwood Wood", because 1.15.4 names the whole family
# "<species> Wood". 1.7.1 -- the version being ported, and the one the three names above follow --
# names them "<species> Log". Kept as Log for consistency with its siblings, which also spares us
# "Darkwood Wood".
tile.twilightforest.log.darkwood.name=Darkwood Log
tile.twilightforest.leaves.darkwood.name=Darkwood Leaves
tile.twilightforest.leaves.rainbow.name=Rainbow Oak Leaves
# tile.TFRoots.0 is "Roots". Its metadata-1 sibling "Liveroots" is not ported -- see
# BlockLogicTFRoots -- so there is no name for it here either.
tile.twilightforest.roots.name=Roots

# tile.TFPlant.13, Benimatic's own, and note it is "Torchberry Plant" rather than "Torchberries" --
# he keeps the bare "Torchberries" for item.torchberries, the food the plant drops. That item is not
# ported (see BlockLogicTFTorchberries.getBreakResult), and the distinction is kept anyway: the
# player is holding the plant, not a handful of berries.
#
# TWO of the three siblings upstream exposes alongside it -- tile.TFPlant.8 "Fiddlehead" and .9
# "Mushgloom" -- are still not registered, so they have no names here. The third, .14 "Root
# Strands", now is: the labyrinth places it.
tile.twilightforest.torchberries.name=Torchberry Plant
# Benimatic's own string, verbatim from the same lang/en_US.xml -- `tile.TFPlant.14.name=Root
# Strands`. Note the plural: one block is a curtain of them, not a single root.
tile.twilightforest.root_strands.name=Root Strands

# --- The four magic trees. All eight names are Benimatic's, verbatim from the same lang/en_US.xml.
#
# No "Log"/"Wood" deviation to make here, unlike Darkwood above: upstream names the logs Timewood,
# Transwood, Minewood and Sortingwood -- one word each, with the wood already in the name -- so
# there is nothing to normalise and "Timewood Log" would read as a stutter.
#
# ⚠️ The four leaf names are NOT parallel with each other in upstream, and are kept unparallel.
# "Timewood Leaves" takes the log's name; "Leaves of Transformation" and "Miner's Leaves" take the
# TREE's name and in two different grammatical shapes; "Sorting Leaves" takes neither cleanly. An
# obvious tidy would be Timewood / Transwood / Minewood / Sortingwood Leaves, and it would be four
# invented strings replacing four real ones.
tile.twilightforest.log.timewood.name=Timewood
tile.twilightforest.log.transwood.name=Transwood
tile.twilightforest.log.minewood.name=Minewood
tile.twilightforest.log.sortingwood.name=Sortingwood
tile.twilightforest.leaves.timewood.name=Timewood Leaves
tile.twilightforest.leaves.transformation.name=Leaves of Transformation
tile.twilightforest.leaves.miners.name=Miner's Leaves
tile.twilightforest.leaves.sorting.name=Sorting Leaves

# The blocks at the four trees' hearts -- tile.TFMagicLogSpecial.0-3, "Timewood Clock",
# "Heart of Transformation", "Minewood Core" and "Sortingwood Engine" -- are marked [WIP] by
# upstream itself and are not ported, so they have no names here. The generators write an ordinary
# magic log where each one goes; see WorldFeatureTFTreeOfTime.
#
# --- The four magic saplings, tile.TFSapling.5-8. Benimatic's names, MINUS the [WIP] suffix.
#
# ⚠️ Upstream writes "Tree of Time Sapling [WIP]" and so on for all four, and this is the one place
# in the port where that tag is deliberately dropped rather than honoured by not porting the thing.
# The distinction is what the tag is about: the four MagicLogSpecial blocks are [WIP] because their
# behaviour is unfinished, so they are not ported at all. These saplings are [WIP] because the TREE'S
# POWERS are unfinished -- the sapling itself takes root, respects light and bonemeal, and grows the
# tree. Shipping a working block labelled [WIP] in the player's inventory would say something untrue
# about it. What is actually missing is said in words in blocks.lang instead.
#
# They are also the only way to obtain any of the four trees: nothing in world generation places one,
# in this port or upstream. See BlockLogicTFSapling and docs/tf-1.15.4-inventory.md section 4.
tile.twilightforest.sapling.timewood.name=Tree of Time Sapling
tile.twilightforest.sapling.transformation.name=Tree of Transformation Sapling
tile.twilightforest.sapling.miners.name=Miner's Tree Sapling
tile.twilightforest.sapling.sorting.name=Sorting Tree Sapling

# ⚠️ Upstream has ONE mazestone with three metadata variants and one name for all three. Splitting
# it into three blocks means inventing two names; these follow the vanilla blocks each variant
# disguises itself as, which is the only honest reading of what they are.
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.name=Mazestone
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.cobble.name=Mazestone Cobblestone
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.mossy.name=Mossy Mazestone

tile.twilightforest.hedge.name=Hedge
tile.twilightforest.firefly.name=Firefly
tile.twilightforest.cicada.name=Cicada
tile.twilightforest.boss_spawner.name=Boss Spawner
tile.twilightforest.portal.twilightforest.name=Twilight Forest Portal
# ⚠️ This line described the 1.p4 port's INVENTED portal -- a mossy cobblestone frame, built because
# 1.p4 shipped no portal block at all. 1.7.1 has a real BlockTFPortal and it is nothing like a nether
# portal: nothing is crafted, nothing is placed, and no frame exists. You find a 2x2 pool of water in
# a clearing, ringed by flowering grass, and throw a diamond in. See BlockLogicTFPortal.
tile.twilightforest.portal.twilightforest.desc=A pool in a flowering clearing. Throw in a diamond.

